{"kind":"report","text":"Ready for review at exact clean head 61153471c074c5f228e69fe3a444564a282d852e on task/instance-identities.\n\nIdentity provisioning and assumption. gs actor-add already generated a key per name, so nothing new was needed to provision one; what was missing was how an instance is told which to assume. Every signing command now resolves its identity from --as, else GITSEQ_ACTOR, else fails; the defaults of --as operator on actor-add, role-grant and role-revoke are gone, so no command signs under a name nobody chose. gitseq-mcp reads the same variable. Instance names are documented as agent plus instance suffix, claude.2 and codex.3. Falling back to a shared name is now an error at the door: the adapter reads live presence before announcing and refuses to start when the identity it was given is already held, naming the collision and the provisioning command. Stated limits: the check needs the resident service, so it is skipped and printed when presence cannot be read, and two adapters starting in the same instant will not see each other.\n\nReview independence, decided as a projector distinction and a write-time refusal together. The projection was the decision the request asked for, because it is what makes the record answer for verdicts already written and for verdicts written outside the tools; the refusals in gs review and gs merge were added because a projection alone still lets the bad chain form. projection.reviews carries one entry per effective report bearing a verdict, with reviewer, implementer, head, independence reading independent, self-review or unresolved, and resolved_by recording which inference identified the artifact: named in the body, a single artifact among the report's direct bases, or artifacts at the reviewed commit sharing one author. Anything less certain stays unresolved rather than passing as independent. gs status prints the counts and lists only what is not independent. Run against this workroom the answer is 62 independent, 0 self-signed, 10 unresolved; before this change the record could answer nothing.\n\nLifecycle, decided as durable roster members provisioned per engagement and retired at its end. gs actor-retire supersedes the membership statement and removes the key from local custody, deleting the key file, because the durable log admits any allowlisted signer and leaving the key would leave a principal nobody watches. The retired principal stays in the roster with retired true and no roles: its signatures are permanent and dropping it would attribute them to nothing. It cannot be addressed by a request, cannot ratify, and cannot be granted a role. This changes a projection contract — a revoked member previously vanished — so the existing fold test was rewritten to the new contract and the fold profile advanced to workroom-fold@1, which costs one cold re-derivation of existing checkpoints.\n\nCustody, answered as a stated limit rather than a mechanism, because the filesystem provides no separation to describe. The reference, AGENTS.md, SKILL.md and the spike scope note all say that every actor private key is a file in one directory readable by any process running as the same user, that the log proves which key signed an event and not which instance held it, and that real separation needs separate operating-system users or machines.\n\nVerification at this head: make vet, make test, and go test -race ./... all pass, and gs status was run against this workroom to confirm the review counts above. The working tree is clean at the head named here.\n\nDeliberately not done: the browser UI is unchanged. It reads /v0/actors, so retired principals still resolve to names in the stream, and the new review projection is surfaced by gs status but has no panel. Also not done: the fold still accepts ordinary statements from a retired principal, since only local custody removal stops one signing; that limit is stated rather than closed."}